+---
+title: Drum Synth
+---
+
+The drum synth is an oscillator type for synthesised drums. It is a port of the drum voices from the open source LXR-02 firmware by Erica Synths, adapted to run inside a Deluge sound. The Deluge's own envelopes, filters, FX, mod matrix, automation and parameter locks all work on top of it.
+
+It has sixteen engines and variants, all chosen in **SYNTH MODE**. The first three are ported from the LXR-02:
+
+- **Kick / tom**: a main oscillator with a pitch envelope, plus a mod oscillator that either frequency modulates it or is mixed with it.
+- **Snare**: a pitched oscillator with a pitch envelope, plus sample-and-hold noise through its own low, high or band pass filter.
+- **Cymbal / hat**: a main oscillator frequency modulated by two more oscillators, one of them usually noise.
+
+**Drom1** and **Drom2** (three variants each) come from the XIA engine set made for the Elektron Model:Cycles. See [Drom1 and Drom2](#drom1-and-drom2). The eight **Cyc engines** are modelled on the Model:Cycles machines. See [Cyc engines](#cyc-engines).
+
+Every engine also has a **transient**: a pitch "snap", a start-phase "offset" click, or one of twelve short click samples.
+
+## Making a drum synth kit
+
+1. In clip view on an empty clip, press **SHIFT + KIT**.
+2. The **New kit** menu opens. Turn **SELECT** to **Drum synth** and press **SELECT** (or **KIT**). Choose **Kit** for a normal, sample based kit.
+3. You get seven rows, from the bottom: **KICK**, **SNARE**, **CH** (closed hat), **OH** (open hat), **LTOM**, **HTOM** and **CYM**.
+
+The two hat rows are in choke mode, so an open hat is cut off by the next closed hat.
+
+You can also turn any row of any kit, or any synth, into a drum synth: press the **SYNTH MODE** shortcut and choose an engine. That way sampled and synthesised drums can share a kit. See [Choosing an engine](#choosing-an-engine).
+
+## Pad editor
+
+On a drum synth row, open the sound menu and choose **Drum synth** (its first item), or press **SELECT** on **OSC1 > TYPE** in the sound menu. The pad editor opens: every pad column is one setting, shown as a level meter from the bottom row (lowest) to the top row (highest).
+
+- **Tap a pad** to set that column to that level. The screen shows the setting's name and value.
+- **Turn SELECT** to fine tune the column you last touched.
+- **Turn the horizontal encoder** to move between columns without changing anything.
+- **Audition pads** still play the row, so you can hold one and tap settings while you listen.
+- **SELECT press** opens the full drum synth menu below, with Load preset.
+- **BACK** goes up to the normal sound menu. The pad editor is its first item, **Drum synth**.
+- **SHIFT** shows the usual grid shortcuts while held.
+
+Single-choice settings (waves, osc mode, noise filter) light one pad per option instead of a meter. The transient column runs from Snap at the bottom, through Offset, to Click 12 at the top.
+
+Columns are coloured by section: white for the main osc, orange for the pitch envelope, light blue for the mod osc and FM, dark blue for the second mod osc, lime for the snare noise, pink for the transient, and green for the Deluge's own controls.
+
+| Column | Kick / tom | Snare | Cymbal / hat |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Osc wave | Osc wave | Osc wave |
+| 2 | Pitch amount | Pitch amount | FM amount |
+| 3 | Pitch decay | Pitch decay | Mod wave |
+| 4 | Pitch curve | Pitch curve | Mod pitch |
+| 5 | Osc mode | Noise mix | Mod fine |
+| 6 | FM amount | Noise rate | FM 2 amount |
+| 7 | Mod wave | Noise filter | Mod 2 wave |
+| 8 | Mod pitch | Noise cutoff | Mod 2 pitch |
+| 9 | Mod fine | Noise resonance | Mod 2 fine |
+| 10 | Transient | Transient | Transient |
+| 11 | Transient level | Transient level | Transient level |
+| 12 | Transient speed | Transient speed | Transient speed |
+| 13 | Tune (osc 1 transpose, -24 to +24 semitones across the pads) | same | same |
+| 14 | Amp decay | same | same |
+| 15 | LPF cutoff | same | same |
+| 16 | HPF cutoff | same | same |
+
+## Drum synth menu
+
+The same settings as a list: press **SELECT** in the pad editor, or go to **OSC1 > TYPE** and press **SELECT**, then **SELECT** again. Settings that the current engine doesn't use are hidden.
+
+| Setting | Engines | What it does |
+|---|---|---|
+| Load preset | all | Replaces the drum synth settings with a starting point. Leaves the Deluge's envelopes, filters and FX alone |
+| Osc wave | all | Main oscillator wave: sine, triangle, saw, square or noise |
+| Pitch amount | kick, snare | Depth of the pitch envelope, up to 32 times the base pitch |
+| Pitch decay | kick, snare | How fast the pitch falls |
+| Pitch curve | kick, snare | Shape of the fall. 64 is linear, lower falls away faster |
+| Osc mode | kick | FM: the mod oscillator modulates the main one. Mix: the two are mixed |
+| FM amount | kick, cymbal | FM depth, or mod oscillator level in mix mode |
+| Mod wave, Mod pitch, Mod fine | kick, cymbal | The mod oscillator. Pitch is in semitones above the main oscillator, 64 = unison |
+| FM 2 amount, Mod 2 wave, Mod 2 pitch, Mod 2 fine | cymbal | The second mod oscillator |
+| Noise mix | snare | Noise level against the oscillator |
+| Noise rate | snare | Sample-and-hold rate of the noise. 127 is plain white noise |
+| Noise filter, Noise cutoff, Noise resonance | snare | The snare's own noise filter |
+| Transient | all | Snap, Offset, or Click 1 to 12 |
+| Transient level, Transient speed | all | Level and playback speed of the transient |
+
+Values run 0 to 127, as on the original hardware.
+
+The pitch of a row is set with the usual oscillator transpose and the Deluge's pitch controls, so the drum synth responds to note, pitch automation and the mod matrix like any other oscillator.
+
+## Choosing an engine
+
+Every engine is in one list: press the **SYNTH MODE** shortcut (or **OSC1 TYPE** on a drum synth row, which opens the same list) and turn SELECT:
+
+| Group | Choices |
+|---|---|
+| The Deluge's own | Sample (on a kit row) or Subtractive, FM, Ringmod, 303 (synths only) |
+| LXR voices | Kick / tom, Snare, Cymbal / hat |
+| Drom1 | Drom1 L, Drom1 B, Drom1 C |
+| Drom2 | Drom2 G, Drom2 I, Drom2 H |
+| Cyc engines | Cyc Kik, Cyc Snr, Cyc Metal, Cyc Perc, Cyc Tone, Cyc Chord, Vtg Snr, clap eng |
+
+Choose **Sample** to put a kit row back on its sample.
+
+A row that wasn't a drum synth gets envelope 2 as a short blip for patching to pitch, and an envelope 1 that suits the engine:
+- LXR voices, Drom1 and Drom2: percussive (attack 0, decay 30, sustain 0, release 30). The Deluge envelope shapes the level.
+- Cyc engines: open (attack 0, sustain 50, release 40). They have their own amp decay; turn the Deluge's sustain down to shorten them from outside.
+
+Moving between the LXR voices, or between variants of Drom1 or Drom2, keeps their settings.
+
+## Shortcut pads
+
+All drum engines share one layout, read the way the grid is printed. Only the four oscillator and FM columns change meaning on a drum synth row; the envelope, filter, FX and master pads keep their usual jobs, and so does any pad an engine doesn't use.
+
+- **Columns.** OSC1 is the main voice. OSC2 is the second layer: the snare noise, a rattle, snares, an overtone, a clap tail, or the first operator. MOD1 is the modulator: FM depth and ratio. MOD2 is the pitch sweep, or the second modulator (Cymbal / hat) or third operator (Drom1) on engines without one.
+- **Rows.** LEVEL is an amount, TRANSPOSE a tuning or ratio, TYPE a choice, PULSE WIDTH a shape or fine tune, RETRIGGER the attack, FEEDBACK drive, resonance or curve, and WAVE INDEX a decay. OSC1 NOISE is a noise amount.
+
+| Pad | Kick / tom | Snare | Cymbal / hat | Drom1 | Drom2 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| OSC1 TYPE | SYNTH MODE | SYNTH MODE | SYNTH MODE | SYNTH MODE | SYNTH MODE |
+| OSC1 PULSE WIDTH | Osc wave | Osc wave | Osc wave | | Shape |
+| OSC1 RETRIGGER | Transient type | Transient type | Transient type | Punch | Punch |
+| OSC1 FEEDBACK | Drive | Drive | Drive | Drive | Drive |
+| OSC1 WAVE INDEX | Engine decay | Engine decay | Engine decay | Engine decay | Engine decay |
+| OSC1 NOISE | | | | | Strike |
+| OSC2 LEVEL | | Noise mix | | Blendex 1 | |
+| OSC2 TRANSPOSE | | Noise rate | | Freq 1 | |
+| OSC2 TYPE | Osc mode (FM / mix) | Noise filter | | | |
+| OSC2 PULSE WIDTH | | Noise cutoff | | | |
+| OSC2 RETRIGGER | Transient level | Transient level | Transient level | | |
+| OSC2 FEEDBACK | | Noise resonance | | | |
+| MOD1 LEVEL | FM amount | | FM amount | Blendex 2 | Alloy |
+| MOD1 TRANSPOSE | Mod pitch | | Mod pitch | Freq 2 | Weight |
+| MOD1 TYPE | Mod wave | | Mod wave | | |
+| MOD1 PULSE WIDTH | Mod fine | | Mod fine | | |
+| MOD1 RETRIGGER | Transient speed | Transient speed | Transient speed | | |
+| MOD2 LEVEL | Pitch amount | Pitch amount | FM 2 amount | Blendex 3 | |
+| MOD2 TRANSPOSE | | | Mod 2 pitch | Freq 3 | |
+| MOD2 TYPE | | | Mod 2 wave | | |
+| MOD2 PULSE WIDTH | | | Mod 2 fine | | |
+| MOD2 FEEDBACK | Pitch curve | Pitch curve | | | |
+| MOD2 WAVE INDEX | Pitch decay | Pitch decay | | | |
+
+| Pad | Cyc Kik | Cyc Snr | Cyc Metal | Cyc Perc | Cyc Tone | Cyc Chord | Vtg Snr | clap eng |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| OSC1 PULSE WIDTH | Shape | | | | | Sweep (wave) | | |
+| OSC1 RETRIGGER | Punch | Punch | Punch | Punch | Punch | Punch | Punch | Punch |
+| OSC1 FEEDBACK | Drive | Drive | Drive | Drive | Drive | Drive | Drive | Drive |
+| OSC1 WAVE INDEX | Amp decay | Amp decay | Amp decay | Amp decay | Amp decay | Amp decay | Amp decay | Amp decay |
+| OSC2 LEVEL | | | | Color (overtone) | | | Freq complex | Balance |
+| OSC2 FEEDBACK | | | | | | | | Body char |
+| OSC2 TRANSPOSE | | Color (rattle tune) | | | | Color (voicing) | | Spacing crunch |
+| OSC2 TYPE | | | | | | Shape (chord) | | |
+| OSC2 PULSE WIDTH | | Shape (rattle) | | | | | | |
+| OSC2 RETRIGGER | Contour (knock) | | | | | | | |
+| OSC2 WAVE INDEX | | Contour (noise decay) | | Contour (overtone decay) | | | | Body envelope |
+| MOD1 LEVEL | | | Shape (density) | Shape (modulation) | Shape (FM index) | | Mod envelope | |
+| MOD1 TRANSPOSE | | | Color (ratio spread) | | Color (FM ratio) | | Inharm | |
+| MOD1 FEEDBACK | Color (harmonic drive) | | | | | | | |
+| MOD1 RETRIGGER | | | | | Sweep (index burst) | | | |
+| MOD1 WAVE INDEX | | | Contour (top end decay) | | Contour (index decay) | Contour (wave decay) | | |
+| MOD2 LEVEL | Sweep | Sweep | Sweep | Sweep | | | Pitch sweep | |
+| MOD FX RATE | Sweep | Sweep | Sweep | Sweep | Sweep | Sweep | Pitch sweep | Spacing crunch |
+| MOD FX DEPTH | Contour | Contour | Contour | Contour | Contour | Contour | Mod envelope | Body envelope |
+
+Every engine's continuous settings are Deluge parameters, so they can be parameter locked, automated, MIDI learned and put on gold knobs. They run 0-127, as on the LXR and the Cycles. The choices (waves, transient type, noise filter, osc mode, Punch) are row settings. The full list is in the sound menu's **Drum synth** item.
+
+**Drive and decay.** Every engine has both, on the same pads and gold knobs. Drom1 and Drom2 use their own. On the LXR voices they are new: drive is a soft clipper that gets louder as it goes up and hard clips at the top, decay is the voice's own fade (127 holds). Both start neutral, so kits made before sound the same. The Cyc engines start with drive at 100.
+
+**Gold knobs.** On every drum synth row the **attack / release** pair is **Drive / Decay** instead of the envelope's (the envelope is still in its menu). On the Cyc engines the **mod rate / mod depth** pair is **Sweep / Contour**. Custom 1 tunes the drum. **Custom 2 / Custom 3** (the last pair) carry each engine's two key settings:
+
+| Engine | Custom 2 | Custom 3 |
+|---|---|---|
+| Kick / tom | Pitch amount | FM amount |
+| Snare | Noise mix | Pitch amount |
+| Cymbal / hat | FM amount | FM 2 amount |
+| Drom1 | Blendex 1 | Freq 1 |
+| Drom2 | Weight | Alloy |
+| Cyc engines | Color (Inharm, Body char) | Shape (Freq complex, Balance) |
+
+The other gold knobs keep their usual Deluge jobs. Hold a step and turn a knob to parameter lock it.
+
+**Snappy decay.** On any drum synth row, **Snappy decay** (in the sound menu, under **Drum synth**) makes the amp envelope's decay and release follow the square of the Deluge's usual curve: at every point it has dropped twice as many dB, so the same decay setting sounds about half as long, with a tighter body. It is on for every new drum synth row, saved with the row, and only changes envelope 1. Rows saved with it off stay off.
+
+**Envelope 2.** Every drum synth row starts with envelope 2 as a short blip: attack 0, decay 5, sustain 0, release 0. Patch it to pitch (or osc 1 pitch) for a transient snap.
+
+The LXR settings share parameters between engines: each voice's settings are stored with the row and come back when you switch back. The settings can't be modulated from the mod matrix, because the Deluge only allows that for those parameters in FM mode.
+
+## Drom1 and Drom2
+
+Drom1 and Drom2 are ported from the compiled Model:Cycles firmware they were written for. At the Cycles' own sample rate and block size they produce the original's output sample for sample, which the unit tests check.
+
+- **Drom1**: three sine operators and a carrier. Each operator's **Blendex** moves it from being heard (0) to modulating (127). L: all three modulate the carrier. B: a chain, 1 into 2 into 3 into the carrier. C: a loop, 1 into 2 into 3 and back into 1, with no carrier.
+- **Drom2**: inharmonic partials. G adds six, I adds six with a falling pitch at the start, H chains eight sines.
+- **Freq 1-3**: each operator's tuning, from 24 semitones down at 0, through unison at 64, to 24 up at 127.
+- **Weight** picks Drom2's partial ratios, **Alloy** their levels (or the modulation depth on H), and **Strike** the level of a noise burst at the start of each hit.
+- **Engine decay** is the engine's own decay, on top of the Deluge's envelope. It starts at 127, which holds (about 9 seconds on Drom2), so the Deluge envelope does the shaping. Turning it down brings back the Cycles' own decay, including Drom2 H's brightness fading with it.
+
+**Differences from the Cycles.**
+- The Deluge's envelope shapes the level, and the engines' own low pass is left open: use the Deluge's filters.
+- Engine decay and other once-per-block changes run about 9% slower, because the Deluge runs at 44.1 kHz rather than 48 kHz. Pitch is exact.
+- Blendex 3 runs 0 to 127 like Blendex 1 and 2. On the Cycles it sits on the fine tune slot and mirrors around 64.
+- Drom2's attack setting is split: below the middle it set the strike level, which is **Strike** here, and above it a fade-in, which is the Deluge envelope's attack.
+
+## Cyc engines
+
+**Cyc Kik**, **Cyc Snr**, **Cyc Metal**, **Cyc Perc**, **Cyc Tone**, **Cyc Chord**, **Vtg Snr** and **clap eng** are named after the Model:Cycles machines (and two Syntakt machines moved into it by Modded-Cycles) that they are modelled on, and they keep those machines' knob names and starting values.
+
+They are new code, not ports. They were written from general synthesis principles and tuned against renders of the original machines: pitch sweeps, decay times, harmonic content and levels were measured knob by knob and matched. They sound close, not identical.
+
+| Engine | Knob 1 | Knob 2 | Knob 3 | Knob 4 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Cyc Kik | Color: odd harmonic drive | Shape: rounder, then spiky and buzzy, then noisy around 96 | Sweep: pitch drop depth and speed | Contour: knock at the start |
+| Cyc Snr | Color: rattle tuning | Shape: rattle from tonal to noise | Sweep: body pitch drop | Contour: how fast the noise dies, leaving a tone |
+| Cyc Metal | Color: spread of the partials | Shape: density | Sweep: pitch drop | Contour: how fast the top end dies |
+| Cyc Perc | Color: bright overtone, 3 to 7 times the note | Shape: modulation, hollow to gnarly | Sweep: pitch drop | Contour: how fast the overtone dies |
+| Cyc Tone | Color: FM ratio, knob / 12 (24 = 2) | Shape: FM index | Sweep: index burst at the start | Contour: how fast the index fades |
+| Cyc Chord | Color: voicing (unison, close, inversions, dropped, spread) | Shape: the chord, 38 of them | Sweep: wave, sine to hollow to buzzy | Contour: how fast the wave mellows |
+| Vtg Snr | Inharm: body ratio | Freq complex: snares against body | Pitch sweep | Mod envelope: knock in the body |
+| clap eng | Body char: brightness and body resonance | Balance: tail against claps | Spacing crunch: gap between claps, then grit | Body envelope: how fast the tail darkens |
+
+The Shape knob on Cyc Chord runs 0 to 127 like the others, stepping through the 38 chords in the Cycles' order: root and fifth, unison, minor, major, sus2, sus4, m7, 7, m(maj7), maj7, 7sus4, dim7, m(add9), add9, m6, 6, and on through the altered chords to fourths and fifths.
+
+**Punch** squares up and lifts the attack and stretches the decay a little.
+
+**Level and defaults.** Each engine is levelled to start close to full scale, then driven: **Drive** starts at 100, a soft clipper after the amp decay that gets louder as it goes up, pushing into hard clipping at 127, and cleaning up as the sound dies away. The amp decay starts longer than the original machines' (for example 50 on Cyc Kik, 60 on Cyc Snr, 80 on Cyc Chord).
+
+**Differences from the originals.**
+- Same knobs, same starting values, new sound engines inside: the curves behind each knob were matched by measurement, and some knobs do more than the original's (Cyc Kik's Contour barely did anything on the Cycles; here it sets the knock).
+- They run natively at 44.1 kHz, so pitch and decay times are exact.
+- No machine-wide gate: notes end with the Deluge envelope.
+
+## CPU load
+
+**SHIFT + SELECT > CPU load** shows the audio engine's load while you play:
+
+- **Load**: audio render time as a percentage of real time.
+- **Peak**: the highest Load since the page was opened.
+- **Voice**: average nanoseconds per sample for one voice, filters and envelopes included.
+- **Drum**: average nanoseconds per sample for the drum synth oscillator alone.
+
+One sample lasts 22,676 ns, so a Voice reading of 900 means each playing voice uses about 4% of the CPU. Measured on hardware, a kick / tom drum synth voice costs about as much as a sample voice.
+
+## Compatibility
+
+Drum synth rows saved by earlier test builds of this drum synth load with their choices intact, but their continuous settings (pitch amount, FM amount and so on) moved onto Deluge parameters: reload a preset or set them again.
+
+
+Kits that use the drum synth won't play those rows on firmware without it.
+
